WEBVTT

NOTE week02-clip4-relative-positions
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.427 line:2 align:center
Chest pain means the same
thing wherever it appears.

2
00:00:03.427 --> 00:00:08.342 line:2 align:center
Here it moves from positions two and
three to positions seven and eight,

3
00:00:08.342 --> 00:00:14.617 line:2 align:center
but pain is still one position after
chest. What matters is the distance.

4
00:00:14.817 --> 00:00:20.049 line:2 align:center
First, a reminder. An attention score
is the dot product of a query and a key,

5
00:00:20.049 --> 00:00:25.094 line:2 align:center
and a dot product depends on the
angle between the two vectors:

6
00:00:25.094 --> 00:00:29.553 line:2 align:center
the smaller the angle, the higher
the score. For chest and pain,

7
00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:35.083 line:2 align:center
the angle is 15 degrees
and the score is 1.04.

8
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:40.605 line:2 align:center
Rotary position embeddings, or RoPE,
encode position by turning these vectors,

9
00:00:40.605 --> 00:00:43.242 line:2 align:center
15 degrees per position.

10
00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:49.906 line:2 align:center
Chest at position 2 turns 30 degrees
and pain at position 3 turns 45,

11
00:00:49.906 --> 00:00:57.083 line:2 align:center
so the angle between them grows to 30
degrees and the score becomes 0.93.

12
00:00:57.283 --> 00:01:00.915 line:2 align:center
Now move the same phrase to
positions seven and eight.

13
00:01:00.915 --> 00:01:07.005 line:2 align:center
Both vectors turn much further, but the
gap between them is still 30 degrees,

14
00:01:07.005 --> 00:01:13.750 line:2 align:center
so the score is still 0.93. Only the
distance between the tokens matters.

15
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.453 line:2 align:center
If pain comes 4 positions
after chest instead of 1,

16
00:01:18.453 --> 00:01:24.130 line:2 align:center
the gap grows to 75 degrees
and the score drops to 0.28.

17
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:29.321 line:2 align:center
Real models rotate many pairs of
dimensions, each at its own speed,

18
00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:32.683 line:2 align:center
like the waves in the previous clip.

19
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:34.746 line:2 align:center
A simpler idea is ALiBi.

20
00:01:34.746 --> 00:01:40.502 line:2 align:center
It leaves the vectors alone and subtracts
a penalty from each attention score,

21
00:01:40.502 --> 00:01:48.870 line:2 align:center
proportional to distance. For cold, with
a slope of 0.25, the scores minus 0.5,

22
00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:55.459 line:2 align:center
0.2, 1.3, 0.6, and 1.6 become minus 1.5,

23
00:01:55.459 --> 00:02:02.817 line:2 align:center
minus 0.55, 0.8, 0.35, and 1.6.

24
00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:09.641 line:2 align:center
After softmax, the weights shift
toward nearby tokens: from 0.05, 0.1,

25
00:02:09.641 --> 00:02:17.448 line:2 align:center
0.3, 0.15, and 0.4, to 0.02, 0.06,

26
00:02:17.448 --> 00:02:22.883 line:2 align:center
0.24, 0.15, and 0.53.

27
00:02:23.083 --> 00:02:29.128 line:2 align:center
Today, RoPE is used in nearly every open
large language model, including Llama,

28
00:02:29.128 --> 00:02:33.614 line:2 align:center
Qwen, DeepSeek, and Gemma, and even
in newer encoders like ModernBERT.

29
00:02:33.614 --> 00:02:37.077 line:2 align:center
To read longer records
than they were trained on,

30
00:02:37.077 --> 00:02:41.580 line:2 align:center
models stretch the rotation
angles after pretraining.
